.TH tuiconv n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuiconv
.PP
\fBtuiconv\fR provides small encoding conversion helpers in pure Tcl.
.CS
package require tclutils::tuiconv

set text [::tclutils::tuiconv::readFile data-cp1252.txt cp1252]
::tclutils::tuiconv::writeFile data-utf8.txt $text utf-8
.CE
.SS Commands
.CS
::tclutils::tuiconv::encodings
::tclutils::tuiconv::convert data fromEncoding toEncoding ?-replace 0|1?
::tclutils::tuiconv::readFile filename encoding ?-replace 0|1?
::tclutils::tuiconv::writeFile filename text encoding ?-replace 0|1?
::tclutils::tuiconv::convertFile infile outfile fromEncoding toEncoding ?-replace 0|1?
.CE
.PP
All file operations use binary channels internally and perform encoding conversion explicitly.
.PP
Conversion is strict, the same under Tcl 8.6 and 9 (0.2):
.TS
l l.
Case	Error code
a character the target encoding cannot hold (\fB€\fR in \fBiso8859\-1\fR)	\fBTCLUTILS TUICONV UNMAPPABLE enc\fR
bytes that are not valid in the source encoding	\fBTCLUTILS TUICONV INVALID enc\fR
unknown encoding	\fBTCLUTILS TUICONV ENCODING enc\fR
.TE
.PP
\fB\-replace 1\fR substitutes instead: \fB?\fR when writing, a replacement character when reading. \fBwriteFile\fR converts before it writes, so on an error the file keeps its content; the bytes go through a temporary file (\fBcommon::replaceFile\fR), so a failed write keeps it too. Up to 0.1 Tcl 8.6 wrote \fB?\fR without a word and Tcl 9.0 raised a plain Tcl error.
